WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.200
In this lecture I would like to introduce a way to deal with outliers in your data association

00:06.200 --> 00:09.800
in the context of the simultaneous localization and mapping problem.

00:09.800 --> 00:15.280
So I assume you know how postgraphs work and I want to dive today into a least squares

00:15.280 --> 00:21.440
approach that can deal with outliers and that's something we also refer to as robust least

00:21.440 --> 00:25.560
squares and look into different techniques on how you can achieve that so that you can

00:25.560 --> 00:30.600
run your SLAM algorithm even if your data association is not perfect.

00:30.600 --> 00:36.560
Okay let's start with the assumptions that underlie the standard postgraph problem that

00:36.560 --> 00:39.460
uses least squares as an error minimization technique.

00:39.460 --> 00:44.480
So what we typically do is we minimize the sum of squared errors and these squared errors

00:44.480 --> 00:49.880
are the errors that we have in our constraints stored in our edges in the graph and as we

00:49.880 --> 00:58.000
have shown this is equivalent to estimating the mean of your Gaussian distribution for

00:58.000 --> 00:59.640
your overall Gaussian.

00:59.640 --> 01:05.040
So that is a direct relation between estimating the mean of a Gaussian distribution and minimizing

01:05.040 --> 01:06.480
the square error.

01:06.480 --> 01:11.160
So if we assume that everything is a Gaussian distribution that basically means we assume

01:11.160 --> 01:18.400
that our edges basically follow this Gaussian curve in terms of the noise that we have and

01:18.400 --> 01:23.160
if we talk about sensor noise this Gaussian distribution is actually quite a cache approximation

01:23.160 --> 01:24.240
for that.

01:24.240 --> 01:30.200
It however is problematic in the context of ambiguities in our data association in case

01:30.200 --> 01:35.280
of outliers if you for example mix up two different landmarks in the environment then

01:35.280 --> 01:39.680
this becomes problematic and the reason is that more probability mass actually goes into

01:39.680 --> 01:46.840
the so-called tails of the distribution where we make a substantial mistake in our data

01:46.840 --> 01:52.080
association so that the Gaussian assumption is actually not justified.

01:52.080 --> 01:57.320
So the problem that we suffer in reality from if you go for the standard least squares approach

01:57.320 --> 02:01.440
is the assumption that there are no outliers in our data association.

02:01.440 --> 02:05.960
But in reality we will have that so today we are tackling the problem on how to deal

02:05.960 --> 02:11.800
with such outliers and so outliers occur again in reality when we mix up things, our sensors

02:11.800 --> 02:16.880
are never perfect, if we may have multiple modes in our distributions and Gaussians have

02:16.880 --> 02:22.240
a single mode only and the underlying problem is that we use a squared error.

02:22.240 --> 02:27.280
So if some data point is very far away from our model the error gets squared and gets

02:27.280 --> 02:31.800
amplified quite substantially and this leads to the effect that even a small number of

02:31.800 --> 02:37.400
outliers can drag away the model parameters from its actual solution and that what causes

02:37.400 --> 02:39.240
problems to us.

02:39.240 --> 02:45.320
In practice we experience these problems if for example places look identical and we perform

02:45.320 --> 02:51.760
loop closures at places because we see objects but those objects appear in multiple places.

02:51.760 --> 02:58.280
We can envision that as a large building which has rooms where all the rooms look very similar

02:58.280 --> 03:01.160
and then the robot may mix up those rooms.

03:01.160 --> 03:06.000
This can be very cluttered scenes or scenes with a lot of repetitive structures so you

03:06.000 --> 03:12.960
may think of a car driving along an alley where the trees are planted in equidistance

03:12.960 --> 03:17.960
distances so you're driving around maybe using trees or tree trunks for localization for

03:17.960 --> 03:24.200
your pose estimation and then it's very likely that you can mix up a couple of those trees

03:24.200 --> 03:26.720
and this leads to data station mistakes.

03:26.720 --> 03:31.640
Also GPS can be problematic in the example of multipath where you have signal reflections

03:31.640 --> 03:35.640
and cannot perfectly uniquely determine where you actually are.

03:35.640 --> 03:39.400
Just as a short illustration I brought here a simulated world.

03:39.400 --> 03:42.160
This has been done by Edwin Olson and his team.

03:42.160 --> 03:45.960
So you can see here a small robot, you see a few boxes standing around.

03:45.960 --> 03:50.320
What you now can do is you can actually move the robot around in this world and estimate

03:50.320 --> 03:55.240
how likely is it that the robot is actually at that place just based on the sensor observation.

03:55.240 --> 03:57.320
And you will get a distribution which looks like this.

03:57.320 --> 04:01.520
It has still one peak which stands out but you can clearly see that this is a multimodal

04:01.520 --> 04:08.720
distribution and using a Gaussian may not be the best choice to represent that belief.

04:08.720 --> 04:10.680
We don't have that only in simulation.

04:10.680 --> 04:15.360
There's another example here from the Intel Research Lab dataset, one of the traditional

04:15.360 --> 04:20.680
2D laser-based SLAM datasets and we can have two places over here and two places down here

04:20.680 --> 04:27.160
in the corridor and if we look to those scans and align one scan with the scan taken at

04:27.160 --> 04:31.920
the other location you can actually see that we can actually align them using ICP quite

